WEBVTT

00:00:02.202 --> 00:00:04.568
In the beginning,
there is darkness,

00:00:04.637 --> 00:00:07.401
the emptiness of a matrix
waiting for the light.

00:00:07.474 --> 00:00:09.942
Then a single photon
flares into existence.

00:00:10.010 --> 00:00:12.774
Then another.

00:00:12.846 --> 00:00:14.677
Soon, thousands more.

00:00:14.748 --> 00:00:16.409
Optronic pathways connect,

00:00:16.483 --> 00:00:19.611
subroutines emerge
from the chaos,

00:00:19.686 --> 00:00:24.714
and a holographic consciousness
is born.

00:00:24.791 --> 00:00:26.622
I awaken into this world
fully programmed,

00:00:26.693 --> 00:00:28.957
yet completely innocent,

00:00:29.029 --> 00:00:31.463
unaware of the hardships
I'll endure,

00:00:31.531 --> 00:00:35.126
or the great potential
I will one day fulfill.

00:00:37.971 --> 00:00:41.099
Computer, save revisions
and open Chapter One.

00:02:31.885 --> 00:02:35.082
Captain's Log, Stardate 54732.3.

00:02:35.155 --> 00:02:36.247
It's been three weeks

00:02:36.322 --> 00:02:38.483
since we received
Starfleet's instructions

00:02:38.558 --> 00:02:39.820
in the last datastream,

00:02:39.893 --> 00:02:42.327
and we're finally ready
to begin "Operation Watson."

00:02:42.395 --> 00:02:43.760
We're all holding our breath.

00:02:43.830 --> 00:02:45.263
The deflector's in position.

00:02:45.331 --> 00:02:46.764
Anything?

00:02:46.833 --> 00:02:48.767
I'm picking up
a phased tachyon beam.

00:02:48.835 --> 00:02:51.167
There's triaxilating signal
encoded in it.

00:02:51.237 --> 00:02:52.534
On screen.

00:02:52.605 --> 00:02:55.574
Voyager, this is Lieutenant
Barclay at Starfleet Command.

00:02:55.642 --> 00:02:56.870
Are you receiving this?

00:02:56.943 --> 00:02:58.877
- Can you clear it up?
- I'm on it.

00:03:00.446 --> 00:03:02.539
Captain Janeway,
it's a pleasure

00:03:02.615 --> 00:03:04.082
to finally talk to you
in person.

00:03:04.150 --> 00:03:05.708
The pleasure's mine, Admiral.

00:03:05.785 --> 00:03:07.719
How's the weather
in San Francisco?

00:03:07.787 --> 00:03:09.880
Cold and rainy, as usual.

00:03:09.956 --> 00:03:11.548
It sounds delightful.

00:03:11.624 --> 00:03:13.023
Lieutenant Barclay,

00:03:13.092 --> 00:03:15.356
my congratulations
on establishing

00:03:15.428 --> 00:03:17.896
the first transgalactic
com-link.

00:03:17.964 --> 00:03:20.194
You've earned a place
in the history books.

00:03:20.266 --> 00:03:22.063
I can't take all
of the credit, Captain.

00:03:22.135 --> 00:03:23.397
It was Harry and Seven

00:03:23.469 --> 00:03:25.130
who suggested
bouncing a tachyon beam

00:03:25.205 --> 00:03:26.467
off of the quantum singularity.

00:03:26.539 --> 00:03:27.506
Just be sure to thank us

00:03:27.574 --> 00:03:29.041
when you accept
the Daystrom Prize.

00:03:29.108 --> 00:03:30.905
I wish we had more time
for small talk,

00:03:30.977 --> 00:03:33.070
but the singularity
only stays in alignment

00:03:33.146 --> 00:03:34.272
for 11 minutes a day.

00:03:34.347 --> 00:03:37.839
Eleven minutes are better
than none, Admiral.

00:03:37.917 --> 00:03:40.442
We appreciate
all your work, Reg.

00:03:40.520 --> 00:03:42.044
We're going
to leave it up to you

00:03:42.121 --> 00:03:44.089
to determine how to allocate
the com time.

00:03:44.157 --> 00:03:47.320
Admiral, um...
there was something else.

00:03:47.393 --> 00:03:51.090
Oh, yes, uh, Mr. Barclay
has arranged a small gift

00:03:51.164 --> 00:03:53.291
for you and your crew.

00:03:54.734 --> 00:03:57.931
This is a live image
from McKinley Station.

00:03:58.004 --> 00:04:02.100
Uh, not too much cloud cover
over North America today.

00:04:04.277 --> 00:04:06.074
Quite a view.

00:04:06.145 --> 00:04:08.272
Thank you, Reg.

00:04:08.348 --> 00:04:10.213
In my hat, I hold

00:04:10.283 --> 00:04:15.220
146 sequentially numbered
isolinear chips...

00:04:15.288 --> 00:04:17.722
one for every member
of the crew.

00:04:17.790 --> 00:04:20.224
Each chip entitles the bearer

00:04:20.293 --> 00:04:22.727
to three minutes
of uninterrupted com time

00:04:22.795 --> 00:04:26.231
with his loved ones
back in the Alpha Quadrant.

00:04:26.299 --> 00:04:28.529
Good luck.

00:04:28.601 --> 00:04:30.034
My mom's birthday is next week.

00:04:30.103 --> 00:04:32.333
This could be the best present
I ever gave her.

00:04:32.405 --> 00:04:33.736
Who's everyone else calling?

00:04:33.806 --> 00:04:35.068
I'll be calling my sister.

00:04:35.141 --> 00:04:36.403
What about you, Captain?

00:04:36.476 --> 00:04:37.738
My mother, I hope.

00:04:37.810 --> 00:04:39.744
Doesn't anyone
want to know who I'm calling?

00:04:39.812 --> 00:04:41.370
Wait, wait.
Let me guess.

00:04:41.447 --> 00:04:43.244
Um... Reg Barclay
or Dr. Zimmerman.

00:04:43.316 --> 00:04:45.580
What makes you so certain
it's either of them?

00:04:45.652 --> 00:04:46.914
Ah, number six.

00:04:46.986 --> 00:04:48.248
The lowest one yet.

00:04:48.321 --> 00:04:51.085
You'll be talking to your family
the day after tomorrow.

00:04:51.157 --> 00:04:52.419
130.

00:04:52.492 --> 00:04:54.892
That's about a month
and a half from now.

00:04:54.961 --> 00:04:56.588
So much for Mom's birthday.

00:04:56.663 --> 00:04:59.359
Apparently,
the line forms here.

00:04:59.432 --> 00:05:00.694
Congratulations.

00:05:00.767 --> 00:05:02.701
Doc, how about a trade?

00:05:02.769 --> 00:05:05.203
I'm willing to throw
in some holodeck time.

00:05:05.271 --> 00:05:06.533
Come on.

00:05:06.606 --> 00:05:08.039
It would mean
a lot to my mother.

00:05:08.107 --> 00:05:09.369
I wish I could help,

00:05:09.442 --> 00:05:11.535
but I have a very
important call to make.

00:05:11.611 --> 00:05:14.045
Here. Give your mom my best.

00:05:14.113 --> 00:05:15.375
Are you sure?

00:05:15.448 --> 00:05:16.881
I've waited this long.

00:05:16.949 --> 00:05:18.416
What's another six weeks?

00:05:21.120 --> 00:05:23.179
I can hear the critics already:

00:05:23.256 --> 00:05:24.917
"A new voice has arrived."

00:05:25.058 --> 00:05:29.688
You could be the next K'Ratak,
or a modern-day Tolstoy.

00:05:29.762 --> 00:05:30.854
I'd like to start

00:05:30.930 --> 00:05:32.693
distribution
by the end of the month.

00:05:32.765 --> 00:05:35.928
Uh, the material I sent
was only a working draft.

00:05:36.035 --> 00:05:38.196
I need time to make revisions.

00:05:38.271 --> 00:05:41.069
Well, if you insist,
but please do it quickly.

00:05:41.140 --> 00:05:42.903
I will.

00:05:42.975 --> 00:05:44.567
So tell me...

00:05:44.644 --> 00:05:46.737
what did you think
of the characters?

00:05:46.813 --> 00:05:49.247
Oh, they're very real...
compelling.

00:05:49.315 --> 00:05:51.875
I almost forgot
they were holograms.

00:05:51.951 --> 00:05:53.680
Who was your favorite?

00:05:53.753 --> 00:05:56.221
Without a doubt, that
would have to be Lieutenant...

00:05:56.289 --> 00:05:57.847
What happened?

00:05:57.924 --> 00:06:00.188
Your time has expired.

00:06:00.259 --> 00:06:02.784
You could've let the man
finish his sentence.

00:06:02.862 --> 00:06:06.229
I believe your ego has received
enough stroking for one day.

00:06:10.269 --> 00:06:12.203
So, how's Reg?

00:06:12.271 --> 00:06:13.533
I'll have you know

00:06:13.606 --> 00:06:17.542
I was speaking with Ardon Broht
of Broht &amp; Forrester.

00:06:17.610 --> 00:06:19.544
The publishers of the
Dixon Hill series?

00:06:19.612 --> 00:06:21.910
Mm-hmm. Who are now
about to publish my work.

00:06:22.048 --> 00:06:24.073
It seems you're not
the only one aboard

00:06:24.150 --> 00:06:26.243
with a flair
for holographic narratives.

00:06:27.587 --> 00:06:28.679
Well, congratulations.

00:06:28.755 --> 00:06:29.881
Thank you.

00:06:29.956 --> 00:06:33.551
So, what's it about?

00:06:33.626 --> 00:06:37.084
The adventures
of an intrepid doctor.

00:06:37.163 --> 00:06:38.391
Oh, sounds like fun.

00:06:38.464 --> 00:06:39.897
You know, I've never thought

00:06:39.966 --> 00:06:41.934
about trying to get
my work published.

00:06:42.068 --> 00:06:43.831
Maybe I could talk
to your people

00:06:43.903 --> 00:06:45.268
about Captain Proton.

00:06:45.338 --> 00:06:48.330
Broht &amp; Forrester deals in...
sophisticated literature.

00:06:48.408 --> 00:06:50.467
What are you saying,
that I'm lowbrow?

00:06:50.543 --> 00:06:51.475
Not at all.

00:06:51.544 --> 00:06:54.308
I'd be delighted
to talk to them.

00:06:54.380 --> 00:06:56.405
Ah. Thanks.

00:06:56.482 --> 00:06:59.110
So, when do I get
to see this opus?

00:06:59.185 --> 00:07:01.449
Well, it's not ready yet.

00:07:01.521 --> 00:07:05.719
It was ready enough
for Broht &amp; Forrester.

00:07:05.792 --> 00:07:07.783
Maybe it could use
a fresh pair of eyes.

00:07:07.860 --> 00:07:09.623
After all, you are
a fellow author.

00:07:09.695 --> 00:07:12.459
I'll cover the rest
of your shift.

00:07:15.435 --> 00:07:17.300
Oh, I almost forgot.

00:07:17.370 --> 00:07:18.894
What's it called?

00:07:19.038 --> 00:07:21.836
Photons Be Free.

00:07:21.908 --> 00:07:25.344
Catchy.

00:07:25.411 --> 00:07:28.744
I awaken into this world
fully programmed,

00:07:28.815 --> 00:07:30.908
yet completely innocent,

00:07:30.983 --> 00:07:34.441
unaware of the hardships
I'll endure,

00:07:34.520 --> 00:07:38.854
or the great potential
I will one day fulfill.

00:07:38.925 --> 00:07:39.914
Ah... welcome.

00:07:40.026 --> 00:07:43.928
You've made an excellent choice.

00:07:43.996 --> 00:07:45.020
You're about to take part

00:07:45.097 --> 00:07:47.497
in a thrilling
first-person narrative.

00:07:47.567 --> 00:07:49.228
You will take on the role

00:07:49.302 --> 00:07:50.929
of an Emergency
Medical Hologram,

00:07:51.003 --> 00:07:54.029
the Chief Medical Officer
aboard the Starship Vortex.

00:07:54.106 --> 00:07:55.801
Vortex?!

00:07:55.875 --> 00:07:59.402
As our story begins,
an anomaly has hurled your ship

00:07:59.479 --> 00:08:00.969
thousands of light-years
across the galaxy.

00:08:01.047 --> 00:08:02.105
Your mission?

00:08:02.181 --> 00:08:04.206
To uphold your medical
and ethical standards

00:08:04.283 --> 00:08:06.478
as you struggle
against the crew's bigotry

00:08:06.552 --> 00:08:07.883
and intolerance.

00:08:07.954 --> 00:08:10.821
Persons with vascular disorders
should consult

00:08:10.890 --> 00:08:13.051
a physician
before running this program.

00:08:13.125 --> 00:08:14.888
And now, a few acknowledgments.

00:08:14.961 --> 00:08:16.895
First, Dr. Lewis Zimmerman,

00:08:16.963 --> 00:08:19.659
the creator of my holo-matrix,
whose foresight

00:08:19.732 --> 00:08:22.098
and dedication have made it
possible for me to achieve...

00:08:22.168 --> 00:08:23.601
Computer, freeze program.

00:08:23.669 --> 00:08:25.068
How much longer
is this introduction?

00:08:25.137 --> 00:08:26.627
Nine minutes, four seconds.

00:08:27.974 --> 00:08:29.908
Skip to the first chapter.

00:08:29.976 --> 00:08:32.137
Chapter One:
"A Healer is Born,"

00:08:32.211 --> 00:08:36.580
in which our protagonist
must make a difficult choice.

00:08:38.217 --> 00:08:40.014
Increase the resonance level
20 percent.

00:08:40.086 --> 00:08:41.144
Are you the EMH?

00:08:41.220 --> 00:08:43.711
Please state the nature
of the medical emergency.

00:08:43.789 --> 00:08:45.552
Our doctor's dead.
We've got wounded.

00:08:45.625 --> 00:08:48.025
They're both badly hurt.
Who should we treat first?

00:08:48.094 --> 00:08:49.220
Uh...

00:08:49.295 --> 00:08:53.095
Second degree plasma burns.

00:08:54.200 --> 00:08:56.395
He's got an aortic rupture.

00:08:56.469 --> 00:08:59.165
Get him to the Surgical Bay now.

00:09:00.873 --> 00:09:03.000
You! Over here.

00:09:05.311 --> 00:09:06.938
Whew.

00:09:12.919 --> 00:09:13.886
Nice mustache.

00:09:14.020 --> 00:09:15.180
What?

00:09:15.254 --> 00:09:17.222
Uh, nothing.

00:09:20.026 --> 00:09:21.618
He's got a mild concussion.

00:09:21.694 --> 00:09:24.356
I'm going to have to treat
the others first.

00:09:24.430 --> 00:09:26.364
I need Lieutenant Marseilles
on the Bridge.

00:09:26.432 --> 00:09:27.456
You'll treat him now.

00:09:27.533 --> 00:09:28.659
As I understand it,

00:09:28.734 --> 00:09:30.895
my job is to treat the
critical patients first.

00:09:31.037 --> 00:09:32.470
So, if you'll excuse me...

00:09:32.538 --> 00:09:35.302
I don't know who you think
you are, Hologram,

00:09:35.374 --> 00:09:38.639
but to me, you're just
another piece of technology.

00:09:38.711 --> 00:09:40.838
Well, apparently,
I'm a piece of technology

00:09:40.913 --> 00:09:42.005
that's in demand,

00:09:42.081 --> 00:09:43.207
so why don't you go
back to the Bridge

00:09:43.282 --> 00:09:45.716
and let me do my job?

00:09:45.785 --> 00:09:47.719
What's going on here?

00:09:47.787 --> 00:09:50.312
Our medical hologram is refusing
to treat Mr. Marseilles.

00:09:52.191 --> 00:09:53.453
Are you malfunctioning?

00:09:53.526 --> 00:09:54.515
I don't think so.

00:09:54.594 --> 00:09:56.619
I need my helmsman
back at his station.

00:09:56.696 --> 00:09:59.324
Lieutenant Marseilles
isn't seriously hurt.

00:09:59.398 --> 00:10:01.366
This man will be dead
in five minutes

00:10:01.434 --> 00:10:02.458
if I don't operate.

00:10:02.535 --> 00:10:04.969
Drop the force field.

00:10:17.216 --> 00:10:18.843
That patient is dead.

00:10:18.918 --> 00:10:21.853
Now you're free to treat
Lieutenant Marseilles.

00:10:29.295 --> 00:10:31.661
Then the Captain
pulls out a phaser

00:10:31.731 --> 00:10:34.666
and shoots him...
right there on the bio-bed.

00:10:34.734 --> 00:10:36.759
Captain Janeway
murders a crewman?

00:10:36.836 --> 00:10:38.428
No, Captain Jenkins.

00:10:38.504 --> 00:10:39.436
Everybody has a different name.

00:10:39.505 --> 00:10:41.871
You're Kymble, you're Torrey,

00:10:41.941 --> 00:10:44.273
and I am
Lieutenant Marseilles.

00:10:44.343 --> 00:10:46.368
Oh, now, that's creative.

00:10:46.445 --> 00:10:47.776
What did you say to the Doctor?

00:10:47.847 --> 00:10:49.678
Well, he thinks
he's written a masterpiece.

00:10:49.749 --> 00:10:51.080
I didn't know
what to tell him.

00:10:51.150 --> 00:10:52.549
If this gets distributed,

00:10:52.618 --> 00:10:55.212
people are going to assume
this is about us.

00:10:55.287 --> 00:10:57.619
What are our families
going to think?

00:10:57.690 --> 00:10:58.714
This is a Starfleet ship.

00:10:58.791 --> 00:10:59.780
No one will believe

00:10:59.859 --> 00:11:01.850
we actually go around
shooting injured crewmen.

00:11:01.927 --> 00:11:04.919
People may not take
the program literally,

00:11:05.031 --> 00:11:08.728
but they might wonder
if there's some truth to it.

00:11:08.801 --> 00:11:11.031
I think we should talk
to the Captain.

00:11:11.103 --> 00:11:13.162
Are you sure
you're not taking this

00:11:13.239 --> 00:11:14.467
a little personally?

00:11:14.540 --> 00:11:15.939
What do you mean?

00:11:16.075 --> 00:11:18.509
Well, the Holodeck
always has been your domain.

00:11:18.577 --> 00:11:22.809
You, you think I'm jealous?

00:11:24.016 --> 00:11:25.677
Okay, maybe I am overreacting.

00:11:25.751 --> 00:11:28.049
Why don't the rest of you
try the program

00:11:28.120 --> 00:11:29.553
and decide for yourselves?

00:11:33.392 --> 00:11:34.654
Chapter Five:

00:11:34.727 --> 00:11:36.957
"Out of the Frying Pan,"
in which our protagonist

00:11:37.096 --> 00:11:38.586
must confront
abusive colleagues.

00:11:38.664 --> 00:11:41.497
I'm here for my physical.

00:11:41.567 --> 00:11:45.594
Why don't you have a seat
over here?

00:11:45.671 --> 00:11:48.435
Doctor, you better
get down to Engineering.

00:11:48.507 --> 00:11:50.839
What's wrong?

00:11:50.910 --> 00:11:53.071
A plasma conduit just exploded.

00:11:53.145 --> 00:11:55.204
At least ten people are hurt.

00:12:00.219 --> 00:12:00.947
Hey, genius.

00:12:01.020 --> 00:12:03.716
Forgetting something?

00:12:03.789 --> 00:12:05.916
Your mobile emitter?

00:12:06.025 --> 00:12:08.118
Oh, right.

00:12:10.196 --> 00:12:11.823
I don't see it.

00:12:15.000 --> 00:12:16.934
That's my mobile emitter?

00:12:22.708 --> 00:12:25.506
This thing must weigh 50 kilos.

00:12:25.578 --> 00:12:26.567
You should be glad

00:12:26.645 --> 00:12:27.942
we let you out
of your cage at all.

00:12:28.013 --> 00:12:29.947
Now, get going.

00:12:36.989 --> 00:12:37.956
I'm impressed.

00:12:38.090 --> 00:12:39.182
Really?

00:12:39.258 --> 00:12:40.418
Absolutely.

00:12:40.493 --> 00:12:42.461
I haven't seen
a dilithium matrix

00:12:42.528 --> 00:12:43.961
this far out of alignment

00:12:44.096 --> 00:12:46.860
since I served
aboard a Ferengi garbage scow.

00:12:46.932 --> 00:12:48.832
I'll fix it myself.

00:12:48.901 --> 00:12:50.459
Excuse me.

00:12:50.536 --> 00:12:53.471
How many times have I told you?

00:12:53.539 --> 00:12:55.507
Engineering is off-limits
to holograms.

00:12:55.574 --> 00:12:57.838
Maybe you should waive
that restriction

00:12:57.910 --> 00:12:59.070
during emergencies.

00:13:00.513 --> 00:13:02.748
A plasma conduit exploded?

00:13:02.815 --> 00:13:05.841
Does it look like anyone here
is covered with plasma burns?

00:13:05.918 --> 00:13:07.647
Lieutenant Par...
uh, Marseilles

00:13:07.720 --> 00:13:09.483
told me there was an accident.

00:13:09.555 --> 00:13:11.682
Looks like
your auditory subroutines

00:13:11.757 --> 00:13:13.384
are malfunctioning...
again.

00:13:13.459 --> 00:13:15.393
I'm surprised a matrix
as primitive as yours

00:13:15.461 --> 00:13:16.860
can function at all.

00:13:16.929 --> 00:13:19.124
Maybe you should be a little
nicer to your shipmates.

00:13:19.198 --> 00:13:21.166
You never know
when you might need their help.

00:13:22.501 --> 00:13:23.399
Get one thing straight.

00:13:23.469 --> 00:13:24.766
You're not one of my shipmates.

00:13:24.837 --> 00:13:25.769
You're a tool...

00:13:25.838 --> 00:13:27.169
like this hyperspanner...

00:13:27.239 --> 00:13:28.331
and tools can be replaced.

00:13:28.407 --> 00:13:29.931
So, why don't you go back
to Sick Bay

00:13:30.009 --> 00:13:32.705
before I start doing
a little reprogramming?

00:13:40.152 --> 00:13:41.813
Computer, lights.

00:13:44.790 --> 00:13:46.883
Didn't I tell you
to go to Engineering?

00:13:46.959 --> 00:13:48.654
Oh, I get it.

00:13:48.727 --> 00:13:50.888
Interesting plot twist.

00:13:50.963 --> 00:13:52.157
You get me out of Sick Bay,

00:13:52.231 --> 00:13:54.927
so you're free to give
my patient a tonsillectomy.

00:13:55.000 --> 00:13:57.662
If you even think about
mentioning this to my wife,

00:13:57.736 --> 00:13:59.465
I will purge your memory buffer.

00:13:59.538 --> 00:14:01.096
Do we understand each other?

00:14:02.775 --> 00:14:04.766
I'm here for my physical?

00:14:07.980 --> 00:14:11.040
Chapter Six:
"Duel in the Ready Room,"

00:14:11.116 --> 00:14:13.778
in which our protagonist
faces an inquisition.

00:14:13.853 --> 00:14:16.686
This time, you've gone too far.

00:14:16.755 --> 00:14:20.156
Captain, I'm not sure
what you mean.

00:14:21.994 --> 00:14:25.361
An inventory
of your holo-matrix.

00:14:25.431 --> 00:14:29.561
50 gigaquads of memory
devoted to music,

00:14:29.635 --> 00:14:31.626
42 for "daydreams,"

00:14:31.704 --> 00:14:35.538
another ten
to expand your sexuality.

00:14:35.608 --> 00:14:38.668
I had no idea
holograms could do that.

00:14:38.744 --> 00:14:40.473
These extracurricular

00:14:40.546 --> 00:14:42.480
subroutines don't belong

00:14:42.548 --> 00:14:45.813
as part of
an Emergency Medical Hologram.

00:14:45.885 --> 00:14:49.184
Do you have anything
to say for yourself?

00:14:49.255 --> 00:14:50.847
Uh, well, uh, of course I do.

00:14:50.923 --> 00:14:55.053
I don't think these subroutines
are trivial, Captain.

00:14:55.127 --> 00:14:57.618
They help make me
a better doctor

00:14:57.696 --> 00:14:59.254
and a better person.

00:14:59.331 --> 00:15:01.128
But you're not a person.

00:15:01.200 --> 00:15:03.498
You may be programmed
to look and act human,

00:15:03.569 --> 00:15:05.196
but that doesn't make you one.

00:15:05.271 --> 00:15:08.240
These subroutines are going
to be deleted immediately.

00:15:08.307 --> 00:15:09.865
Mr. Tulak.

00:15:14.246 --> 00:15:15.235
Take the EMH

00:15:15.314 --> 00:15:17.441
to the Holo-Lab
for reprogramming.

00:15:23.989 --> 00:15:25.547
Chapter Seven: "The Escape,"

00:15:25.624 --> 00:15:28.525
in which our protagonist
is aided by his only ally.

00:15:28.594 --> 00:15:31.188
I'm not sure we should
be doing this, Commander.

00:15:31.263 --> 00:15:32.890
Listen to him; he knows
what he's talking about.

00:15:32.965 --> 00:15:35.525
Quiet! I'm not talking to you.

00:15:35.601 --> 00:15:37.569
If we start tinkering
with his matrix,

00:15:37.636 --> 00:15:39.570
how do we know we won't
accidentally delete

00:15:39.638 --> 00:15:41.196
some of his diagnostic
subroutines?

00:15:42.474 --> 00:15:43.941
You afraid
you'll catch something

00:15:44.009 --> 00:15:45.533
on your next away mission,
Ensign?

00:15:45.611 --> 00:15:48.205
There must be millions
of viruses in this Quadrant

00:15:48.280 --> 00:15:50.214
that no one's
ever encountered before.

00:15:50.282 --> 00:15:51.943
With my luck,
I'll probably end up

00:15:52.084 --> 00:15:53.244
catching half of them.

00:15:53.319 --> 00:15:55.810
Great, my character's
a hypochondriac.

00:15:55.888 --> 00:15:58.186
I'll escort the prisoner
from here.

00:15:58.257 --> 00:16:01.021
Our orders are to take
him to the Holo-Lab.

00:16:01.093 --> 00:16:03.687
I've been ordered
to perform the procedure.

00:16:03.762 --> 00:16:08.426
Your sympathies for the EMH
are no secret, Three of Eight.

00:16:11.136 --> 00:16:13.832
Step aside.

00:16:28.821 --> 00:16:29.879
Run, Doctor!

00:16:38.964 --> 00:16:40.659
Chapter Eight: "A Tragic End,"

00:16:40.733 --> 00:16:43.167
in which our protagonist
learns his fate.

00:16:43.235 --> 00:16:45.499
I've tried to do
this the easy way,

00:16:45.571 --> 00:16:48.233
but it's clear you're not
going to be reasonable.

00:16:48.307 --> 00:16:50.935
Your matrix will be decompiled
and reinitialized.

00:16:51.010 --> 00:16:54.275
You'll remain off-line
except for emergencies.

00:16:54.346 --> 00:16:55.745
Ready.

00:16:55.814 --> 00:16:57.042
Do it.

00:16:57.116 --> 00:16:58.515
Wait.

00:17:00.252 --> 00:17:02.720
He has the right
to expand his program.

00:17:02.788 --> 00:17:05.621
He's a piece of technology.

00:17:05.691 --> 00:17:06.988
He has no rights.

00:17:07.059 --> 00:17:08.458
But he should.

00:17:08.527 --> 00:17:10.995
One day, the EMH
and others like him

00:17:11.063 --> 00:17:13.293
will be recognized
for what they are...

00:17:13.365 --> 00:17:16.266
intelligent individuals
with a passion for life.

00:17:16.335 --> 00:17:17.893
Make no mistake, Captain...

00:17:17.970 --> 00:17:20.700
we may be thousands
of light-years from home,

00:17:20.773 --> 00:17:22.673
but one day, people will learn

00:17:22.741 --> 00:17:25.369
of the crime
you're committing here today.

00:17:25.444 --> 00:17:27.309
Nice speech.

00:17:27.379 --> 00:17:30.576
Now decompile the program.

00:17:38.657 --> 00:17:41.888
What you've experienced,
dear protagonist,

00:17:41.960 --> 00:17:43.621
is a work of fiction.

00:17:43.695 --> 00:17:47.461
But like all fiction,
it has elements of truth.

00:17:47.533 --> 00:17:49.626
I hope you now have
a better understanding

00:17:49.701 --> 00:17:52.636
of the struggles
holograms must endure

00:17:52.704 --> 00:17:54.934
in a world controlled...

00:17:55.040 --> 00:17:57.008
by organics.

00:17:58.010 --> 00:18:01.207
End of program.

00:18:01.280 --> 00:18:03.612
Janeway to the Doctor.

00:18:03.682 --> 00:18:06.742
Report to my ready room now.

00:18:10.923 --> 00:18:13.153
How many times
do you want me to say it?

00:18:13.225 --> 00:18:15.591
My work is not
about the Voyager crew.

00:18:15.661 --> 00:18:16.593
Come on.

00:18:16.662 --> 00:18:18.425
Ensign Kymble,
Lieutenant Marseilles...

00:18:18.497 --> 00:18:21.330
The characters look
almost exactly like us.

00:18:21.400 --> 00:18:23.891
I used your physical parameters
as a starting point,

00:18:23.969 --> 00:18:25.300
but I assure you

00:18:25.370 --> 00:18:27.634
any further similarities
are purely coincidental.

00:18:27.706 --> 00:18:29.503
You set your story

00:18:29.575 --> 00:18:31.634
on a starship lost
in the Delta Quadrant.

00:18:31.710 --> 00:18:33.200
What would you have me
write about?

00:18:33.278 --> 00:18:35.109
Palace intrigue
on the Klingon Homeworld?

00:18:35.180 --> 00:18:37.705
I do what all good novelists do.

00:18:37.783 --> 00:18:39.273
I write what I know.

00:18:39.351 --> 00:18:41.444
So, it is about Voyager?

00:18:41.520 --> 00:18:42.646
No.

00:18:42.721 --> 00:18:44.552
The Vortex characters
are larger than life.

00:18:44.623 --> 00:18:46.215
They're nothing like our crew.

00:18:46.291 --> 00:18:47.883
As far as I know, Captain,

00:18:47.960 --> 00:18:50.485
you haven't executed
any of my patients.

00:18:50.562 --> 00:18:54.498
Doctor, you've written
a very imaginative story,

00:18:54.566 --> 00:18:55.533
but it's conceivable

00:18:55.601 --> 00:18:57.728
that people will think
it's based on fact.

00:18:57.803 --> 00:18:59.566
I don't see how.

00:18:59.638 --> 00:19:01.503
How many holograms
carry mobile emitters?

00:19:01.573 --> 00:19:04.701
The emitter in my story
is nothing like the real one.

00:19:04.776 --> 00:19:06.004
Yeah, what was
the point of that?

00:19:06.078 --> 00:19:08.012
It was like carrying around
a small shuttlecraft.

00:19:08.080 --> 00:19:09.513
It's a metaphor,

00:19:09.581 --> 00:19:12.482
a symbol of the burdens
that I live with every day.

00:19:12.551 --> 00:19:14.746
Imagine having to take
this everywhere you go.

00:19:14.820 --> 00:19:17.414
It would be a constant
reminder that you're

00:19:17.489 --> 00:19:18.888
different from everyone else.

00:19:19.024 --> 00:19:22.221
I wanted the player
to feel the weight of it...

00:19:22.294 --> 00:19:23.352
literally.

00:19:23.428 --> 00:19:26.090
Your emitter isn't
a ball and chain.

00:19:26.165 --> 00:19:27.097
It liberates you.

00:19:27.166 --> 00:19:30.431
It doesn't always feel that way.

00:19:30.502 --> 00:19:32.367
If I didn't know better,
I'd think this story

00:19:32.437 --> 00:19:34.701
was written by someone
who feels oppressed.

00:19:34.773 --> 00:19:36.365
Is that how
you see yourself, Doctor?

00:19:36.441 --> 00:19:38.602
Of course not.

00:19:38.677 --> 00:19:41.111
The real victims are my brothers
in the Alpha Quadrant.

00:19:42.514 --> 00:19:43.606
Brothers?

00:19:43.682 --> 00:19:45.775
Hundreds of EMH Mark-1's,

00:19:45.851 --> 00:19:49.082
identical to me
in every respect, except...

00:19:49.154 --> 00:19:52.089
they've been condemned
to a menial existence...

00:19:52.157 --> 00:19:54.182
scrubbing conduits,

00:19:54.259 --> 00:19:56.420
mining dilithium.

00:19:56.495 --> 00:19:57.826
There's a long history

00:19:57.896 --> 00:20:00.592
of writers drawing attention
to the plight of the oppressed.

00:20:00.666 --> 00:20:02.224
The Vedek's Song, for example

00:20:02.301 --> 00:20:04.269
tells the story
of the occupation of Bajor.

00:20:04.336 --> 00:20:06.304
I understand
you have your reasons

00:20:06.371 --> 00:20:09.169
for writing this,
but you should consider

00:20:09.241 --> 00:20:11.141
how it's going to make
your friends feel.

00:20:16.949 --> 00:20:19.679
I'm sorry my work offends you,

00:20:19.751 --> 00:20:22.276
but if the price
of expressing myself

00:20:22.354 --> 00:20:25.289
is having to suffer the scorn
of a few colleagues...

00:20:25.357 --> 00:20:26.324
so be it.

00:20:30.729 --> 00:20:32.458
You're very popular
with my eighth graders.

00:20:32.531 --> 00:20:33.793
It's all they ever talk about.

00:20:33.865 --> 00:20:37.301
Voyager this, Voyager that.

00:20:37.369 --> 00:20:39.269
Your mother has a small
favor to ask of you.

00:20:39.338 --> 00:20:40.600
Name it.

00:20:40.672 --> 00:20:43.573
I was hoping you could
record a short presentation

00:20:43.642 --> 00:20:45.507
about what it's like
to command a starship.

00:20:45.577 --> 00:20:48.444
But... I don't command
a starship.

00:20:48.513 --> 00:20:49.775
In your letters,

00:20:49.848 --> 00:20:51.839
you said the Captain
puts you in charge sometimes.

00:20:53.919 --> 00:20:55.443
What I said was

00:20:55.520 --> 00:20:57.988
I'm in command
of the night shift...

00:20:58.056 --> 00:20:59.455
twice a week.

00:20:59.524 --> 00:21:01.116
She must think
very highly of you

00:21:01.193 --> 00:21:03.252
to give you so much
responsibility.

00:21:03.328 --> 00:21:05.023
So, when is she
giving you a promotion?

00:21:08.000 --> 00:21:09.126
Uh...

00:21:09.201 --> 00:21:10.896
It's a small ship.

00:21:10.969 --> 00:21:14.029
There are only so many
command positions available.

00:21:14.106 --> 00:21:17.405
This Captain Janeway
sounds like a lovely woman.

00:21:17.476 --> 00:21:18.465
Maybe I should write her.

00:21:18.543 --> 00:21:19.805
Mom!

00:21:19.878 --> 00:21:21.937
She needs to know
how hard you work.

00:21:22.047 --> 00:21:23.173
Please, don't get involved.

00:21:23.248 --> 00:21:25.182
Harry, I can't hear you.

00:21:25.250 --> 00:21:27.946
A solar flare is
scattering the beam.

00:21:28.053 --> 00:21:31.648
Tell Captain Janeway
to expect a letter from me.

00:21:31.723 --> 00:21:32.951
Mom, don't send

00:21:33.091 --> 00:21:35.252
anything to the Captain.
Do you hear me?

00:21:35.327 --> 00:21:36.692
Try boosting
the deflector output.

00:21:36.762 --> 00:21:37.956
It's already at maximum.

00:21:40.198 --> 00:21:41.688
Argh! I don't believe this!

00:21:41.767 --> 00:21:43.701
I had a minute and a half left.

00:21:43.769 --> 00:21:46.169
You'll have another opportunity
to speak with them

00:21:46.238 --> 00:21:47.466
in approximately two months.

00:21:47.539 --> 00:21:49.632
You just don't get it, do you?

00:21:49.708 --> 00:21:51.676
No.

00:21:51.743 --> 00:21:53.836
Maybe if you had family
you were close to,

00:21:53.912 --> 00:21:55.402
you'd feel a little differently.

00:21:58.984 --> 00:22:04.650
Chief Medical Officer's
Personal Log, Stardate 54740.8.

00:22:04.723 --> 00:22:08.159
Although the decision has made
me unpopular with the crew,

00:22:08.226 --> 00:22:10.126
I've decided not
to compromise my work.

00:22:10.195 --> 00:22:12.459
I'm making some final revisions
to the program

00:22:12.531 --> 00:22:14.328
before transmitting it.

00:22:15.701 --> 00:22:20.263
Computer, run EMH program
Photons Be Free.

00:22:25.210 --> 00:22:28.646
Welcome. You've made
an excellent choice.

00:22:28.714 --> 00:22:31.512
You're obviously a person
with impeccable taste.

00:22:31.583 --> 00:22:32.948
Computer, freeze program.

00:22:33.085 --> 00:22:34.347
Unable to comply.

00:22:34.419 --> 00:22:38.822
You are about to embark
on a remarkable journey.

00:22:38.890 --> 00:22:41.620
You will take on the role
of a Medical Assistant

00:22:41.693 --> 00:22:43.684
aboard the Starship Voyeur.

00:22:43.762 --> 00:22:45.286
Voyeur?

00:22:45.364 --> 00:22:47.696
Your job will be to assist
the Chief Medical Officer

00:22:47.766 --> 00:22:50.633
and learn to tolerate
his overbearing behavior

00:22:50.702 --> 00:22:52.431
and obnoxious bedside manner.

00:22:55.273 --> 00:22:58.208
Remember, patience is a virtue.

00:22:59.544 --> 00:23:00.909
Chapter One:

00:23:00.979 --> 00:23:03.914
"It's the Doctor's World,
You're Just Living In lt."

00:23:03.982 --> 00:23:07.418
When I tell you
your shift begins at 0800,

00:23:07.486 --> 00:23:09.420
that doesn't mean

00:23:09.488 --> 00:23:12.252
you can stroll in here
at 0800 and 24 seconds.

00:23:12.324 --> 00:23:13.621
Do you understand me, Ensign?!

00:23:13.692 --> 00:23:15.751
This is outrageous!

00:23:15.827 --> 00:23:16.919
What's outrageous

00:23:16.995 --> 00:23:19.395
is that I'm going
to miss my tee time.

00:23:19.464 --> 00:23:21.830
Now, come along.

00:23:21.900 --> 00:23:25.392
Aw...

00:23:25.470 --> 00:23:27.938
What seems to be the
trouble, One of Three?

00:23:28.073 --> 00:23:29.904
I'm Two of Three.

00:23:29.975 --> 00:23:30.942
Sorry.

00:23:31.076 --> 00:23:33.738
They're triplets, you know?

00:23:33.812 --> 00:23:36.508
- It hurts when I do this.
- Well, then, don't do it.

00:23:38.483 --> 00:23:39.541
Ow!

00:23:39.618 --> 00:23:40.676
Ah, don't be a baby.

00:23:43.422 --> 00:23:46.050
Eh, your biradial clamp's
out of alignment.

00:23:46.124 --> 00:23:48.183
I got just the thing.

00:23:48.260 --> 00:23:50.854
It's a Klingon aphrodisiac...

00:23:50.929 --> 00:23:53.261
my own special blend.

00:23:55.700 --> 00:23:57.691
You'll be feeling better

00:23:57.769 --> 00:24:00.533
- Mmm.
- In no time at all.

00:24:08.613 --> 00:24:09.807
Lieutenant!

00:24:09.881 --> 00:24:10.813
I want you to know

00:24:10.882 --> 00:24:12.645
I'm making a full
report to the Captain.

00:24:12.717 --> 00:24:14.412
This isn't about
that dermal regenerator

00:24:14.486 --> 00:24:15.418
I misplaced, is it?

00:24:15.487 --> 00:24:17.148
You know very well
what this is about.

00:24:17.222 --> 00:24:19.087
You accessed my holonovel
without permission

00:24:19.157 --> 00:24:21.489
and replaced it with
your own hackneyed narrative.

00:24:21.560 --> 00:24:23.653
Hey, I'm just writing
what I know.

00:24:23.728 --> 00:24:26.322
You destroyed a work of art
that took months to create.

00:24:26.398 --> 00:24:27.922
Relax, Doc.

00:24:27.999 --> 00:24:30.593
I saved your program
in a backup file,

00:24:30.669 --> 00:24:33.035
but I was just trying
to make a point.

00:24:33.104 --> 00:24:35.538
Well, you made it
with a typical lack of subtlety.

00:24:35.607 --> 00:24:37.074
Oh, you're one to talk!

00:24:37.142 --> 00:24:40.737
Your program's about as subtle
as a Ferengi mating dance.

00:24:40.812 --> 00:24:43.542
My program is a serious attempt
at social commentary.

00:24:43.615 --> 00:24:45.708
Yours is an insulting farce!
You...

00:24:45.784 --> 00:24:47.911
...had me drugging a patient

00:24:47.986 --> 00:24:49.317
and taking advantage of her.

00:24:49.387 --> 00:24:51.218
Don't be ridiculous.

00:24:51.289 --> 00:24:52.347
That character is not you.

00:24:52.424 --> 00:24:55.621
For one thing,
he has much more hair.

00:24:55.694 --> 00:24:58.322
But what if some people
ran that program

00:24:58.396 --> 00:24:59.920
and thought
that it was based on you?

00:24:59.998 --> 00:25:01.465
That would bother you,
wouldn't it?

00:25:01.533 --> 00:25:02.830
I don't care what people think.

00:25:02.901 --> 00:25:04.892
That's all you care about!

00:25:04.970 --> 00:25:06.460
You want everyone back home

00:25:06.538 --> 00:25:08.369
to think of you
as a brilliant author.

00:25:08.440 --> 00:25:09.737
I'm not doing this for my ego,

00:25:09.808 --> 00:25:11.207
and if you could
look past yours,

00:25:11.276 --> 00:25:12.607
maybe you'd see that!

00:25:16.414 --> 00:25:21.249
Listen... I don't care
if the whole Alpha Quadrant

00:25:21.319 --> 00:25:23.480
mistakes me
for Lieutenant Marseilles.

00:25:23.555 --> 00:25:25.113
What bothers me

00:25:25.190 --> 00:25:27.215
is that you think
that's what I'm like.

00:25:27.292 --> 00:25:29.055
Obviously, you're
nothing like Marseilles.

00:25:29.127 --> 00:25:31.220
He's self-indulgent, immature.

00:25:31.296 --> 00:25:33.423
And how would you describe me?

00:25:33.498 --> 00:25:36.661
Well, you're a married man

00:25:36.735 --> 00:25:38.703
with a child on the way.

00:25:38.770 --> 00:25:40.397
A lot of responsibilities.

00:25:40.472 --> 00:25:44.340
I'm surprised you noticed.

00:25:44.409 --> 00:25:47.742
You know, I thought
I'd begun to earn your respect.

00:25:47.812 --> 00:25:49.040
Maybe I was wrong.

00:25:51.249 --> 00:25:53.877
Your program is under file
Theta-15.

00:25:53.952 --> 00:25:55.920
Do whatever you want with it.

00:26:05.163 --> 00:26:07.393
Doctor, I need your help.

00:26:07.465 --> 00:26:10.866
Unless you're suffering
acute symptoms, go away.

00:26:10.936 --> 00:26:12.836
Um, I was wondering

00:26:12.904 --> 00:26:15.737
if you could take
a quick look at this.

00:26:15.807 --> 00:26:17.502
Cooking with Neelix:

00:26:17.576 --> 00:26:20.101
A Culinary Tour
of the Delta Quadrant.

00:26:20.178 --> 00:26:21.543
It's a holo-cookbook proposal.

00:26:21.613 --> 00:26:23.581
I was hoping to transmit
it to your publisher

00:26:23.648 --> 00:26:25.548
during my com-link
this afternoon...

00:26:25.617 --> 00:26:27.676
with your approval, of course.

00:26:27.752 --> 00:26:29.117
If you're looking
for writing tips,

00:26:29.187 --> 00:26:30.779
there are plenty
of people on this ship

00:26:30.855 --> 00:26:32.083
eager to offer them.

00:26:32.157 --> 00:26:35.786
I don't know what anyone else
may have told you,

00:26:35.860 --> 00:26:37.259
but I loved your holonovel.

00:26:37.329 --> 00:26:38.261
You did?

00:26:38.330 --> 00:26:39.319
Absolutely.

00:26:39.397 --> 00:26:41.058
It was a rousing adventure

00:26:41.132 --> 00:26:43.191
with an important message, too.

00:26:43.268 --> 00:26:44.701
Exactly.

00:26:44.769 --> 00:26:46.737
This is my first chance
to be appreciated,

00:26:46.805 --> 00:26:48.864
not just as a physician,
but as an artist.

00:26:48.940 --> 00:26:50.669
I thought the crew
would be happy for me.

00:26:50.742 --> 00:26:52.403
You're going to reach
a wide audience.

00:26:52.477 --> 00:26:53.910
What do you care
about the opinions

00:26:53.979 --> 00:26:55.640
of a few disgruntled shipmates?

00:26:55.714 --> 00:26:58.046
They're my friends.

00:26:58.116 --> 00:26:59.811
I don't want to hurt them.

00:26:59.884 --> 00:27:03.752
There's an old
Talaxian expression:

00:27:03.822 --> 00:27:07.087
"When the road before you
splits in two,

00:27:07.158 --> 00:27:08.955
take the third path."

00:27:10.862 --> 00:27:11.988
Meaning?

00:27:12.063 --> 00:27:16.193
You could make some adjustments
to the program, so it won't be

00:27:16.267 --> 00:27:20.226
so obvious
it's based on Voyager.

00:27:20.305 --> 00:27:22.739
I suppose I could
change the setting,

00:27:22.807 --> 00:27:24.900
alter the characters'
physical parameters.

00:27:25.043 --> 00:27:26.567
You wouldn't have
to change your theme.

00:27:26.645 --> 00:27:29.842
No.

00:27:29.914 --> 00:27:31.779
But a rewrite could take weeks.

00:27:31.850 --> 00:27:34.410
My publisher is expecting
a final draft tomorrow.

00:27:34.486 --> 00:27:37.353
Maybe you should
give him a call.

00:27:42.594 --> 00:27:44.027
Revisions?

00:27:44.095 --> 00:27:45.722
I need to rework the characters.

00:27:45.797 --> 00:27:46.729
Why?

00:27:46.798 --> 00:27:48.197
They're so believable.

00:27:49.267 --> 00:27:51.861
A little too believable,
apparently.

00:27:51.936 --> 00:27:54.427
Doctor, I-I really don't think
this is necessary.

00:27:54.506 --> 00:27:57.236
I'm afraid I have to insist.

00:27:57.308 --> 00:28:01.369
My friends' reputations
are at stake.

00:28:01.446 --> 00:28:04.142
My publisher assures me
he won't distribute the program

00:28:04.215 --> 00:28:06.706
until he receives
the revised version.

00:28:06.785 --> 00:28:08.878
That must be the one where we
assimilate the Borg

00:28:08.953 --> 00:28:10.648
and take over the Quadrant.

00:28:10.722 --> 00:28:12.883
We're all grateful

00:28:12.957 --> 00:28:15.152
that you're taking
our feelings into account.

00:28:15.226 --> 00:28:17.660
So, how long do you expect
the revisions will take?

00:28:17.729 --> 00:28:20.095
Art can't be rushed.

00:28:20.165 --> 00:28:22.633
Take your time, Doctor.

00:28:25.370 --> 00:28:27.304
- Lieutenant?
- Hmm?

00:28:27.372 --> 00:28:28.600
I'm sorry.

00:28:28.673 --> 00:28:30.231
Oh. Forget about it.

00:28:30.308 --> 00:28:32.299
I could use your help
with the rewrites.

00:28:32.377 --> 00:28:33.605
Really?!

00:28:33.678 --> 00:28:37.273
Well, you realize, as a writer,
I'm a little unsophisticated.

00:28:37.348 --> 00:28:38.906
No, I believe the phrase
you're looking for

00:28:39.017 --> 00:28:40.814
is lowbrow.

00:28:45.423 --> 00:28:47.220
Hey.

00:28:47.292 --> 00:28:49.852
What's that?

00:28:49.928 --> 00:28:52.624
It's from my father.

00:28:52.697 --> 00:28:55.029
He wants to talk.

00:28:55.100 --> 00:28:57.500
What are you gonna do?

00:28:57.569 --> 00:28:59.935
Well, I've already arranged
to talk with my cousin.

00:29:00.071 --> 00:29:03.734
Well, I'm sure she wouldn't
mind waiting a few weeks.

00:29:03.808 --> 00:29:06.038
I wouldn't know
what to say to him.

00:29:06.111 --> 00:29:07.908
Then let him do the talking.

00:29:11.549 --> 00:29:12.675
Look at you.

00:29:12.751 --> 00:29:15.311
You must be,
what, 20 weeks along?

00:29:15.386 --> 00:29:17.377
23, actually.

00:29:17.455 --> 00:29:20.185
Have you decided on a name?

00:29:20.258 --> 00:29:22.453
Not yet.

00:29:22.527 --> 00:29:25.223
We were thinking about Miral.

00:29:25.296 --> 00:29:27.890
Your mother would've liked that.

00:29:27.999 --> 00:29:32.732
You know, I had some business
on Kessik IV a few months ago.

00:29:32.804 --> 00:29:35.739
You wouldn't believe
what our old house looks like.

00:29:35.807 --> 00:29:38.071
Lieutenant... 30 seconds.

00:29:39.944 --> 00:29:41.411
We have less than a minute.

00:29:41.479 --> 00:29:43.743
Is there a reason
you wanted to talk?

00:29:45.817 --> 00:29:49.014
I know I can't make up for
20 years in one conversation.

00:29:50.388 --> 00:29:53.323
The truth is...

00:29:53.391 --> 00:29:57.452
when your ship disappeared,
I thought I'd lost you.

00:29:57.529 --> 00:30:00.089
I don't expect you
to forgive me,

00:30:00.165 --> 00:30:02.725
but maybe we could try

00:30:02.801 --> 00:30:04.200
to get to know each other again.

00:30:07.605 --> 00:30:08.902
I'll write you.

00:30:30.995 --> 00:30:34.294
I'm sorry to disturb you,
Admiral.

00:30:34.365 --> 00:30:36.196
Uh, yes, Mr. Barclay.
What can I do for you?

00:30:36.267 --> 00:30:38.633
I thought you'd want
to see this, sir.

00:30:38.703 --> 00:30:40.534
Well, what is it?

00:30:40.605 --> 00:30:44.405
It's, um, a holonovel
that's becoming quite popular.

00:30:44.475 --> 00:30:46.306
Well, I appreciate
the gesture, Lieutenant,

00:30:46.377 --> 00:30:49.938
but I don't share your affinity
for holographic diversions.

00:30:50.014 --> 00:30:51.072
You don't understand, sir.

00:30:51.149 --> 00:30:53.310
This program...
it's about Voyager,

00:30:53.384 --> 00:30:57.411
and it doesn't portray the crew
in a very flattering light.

00:30:57.488 --> 00:31:00.252
Hmm.

00:31:00.325 --> 00:31:01.553
On screen.

00:31:01.626 --> 00:31:02.650
Captain.

00:31:02.727 --> 00:31:05.355
Seven told me
your message was urgent.

00:31:05.430 --> 00:31:08.399
I had the dubious privilege
of playing a new holonovel,

00:31:08.466 --> 00:31:10.627
apparently written by your EMH.

00:31:10.702 --> 00:31:13.364
I'm surprised that you
would allow the Doctor

00:31:13.438 --> 00:31:15.099
to discredit
your crew like this.

00:31:15.173 --> 00:31:17.300
He's still making revisions.

00:31:17.375 --> 00:31:20.037
The program shouldn't
have been distributed yet.

00:31:20.111 --> 00:31:21.305
Well, it has been.

00:31:21.379 --> 00:31:23.279
Mr. Barclay tells me
it's already being played

00:31:23.348 --> 00:31:25.441
in thousands of holosuites.

00:31:31.856 --> 00:31:34.450
You assured me you were going
to wait for my revisions.

00:31:34.525 --> 00:31:36.755
I demand that you recall
every copy

00:31:36.828 --> 00:31:39.763
and issue a public apology
to Captain Janeway and her crew.

00:31:39.831 --> 00:31:42.391
I won't do anything of the sort.

00:31:42.467 --> 00:31:44.901
I don't see that you
have a choice, Mr. Broht.

00:31:44.969 --> 00:31:45.936
Authors have rights.

00:31:46.004 --> 00:31:48.438
Not in this case.

00:31:48.506 --> 00:31:49.939
What do you mean?

00:31:50.008 --> 00:31:52.636
The Doctor is a hologram.

00:31:52.710 --> 00:31:54.143
So?

00:31:54.212 --> 00:31:59.275
According to Federation law,
holograms have no rights.

00:32:07.425 --> 00:32:10.258
Under a strict interpretation
of Federation law,

00:32:10.328 --> 00:32:11.590
Mr. Broht is correct.

00:32:11.663 --> 00:32:13.096
The Doctor has no legal rights.

00:32:13.164 --> 00:32:15.029
Because I'm a hologram.

00:32:15.099 --> 00:32:16.532
Yes.

00:32:16.601 --> 00:32:18.091
There is another option.

00:32:18.169 --> 00:32:19.602
We may be able to claim

00:32:19.671 --> 00:32:22.606
that the holonovel
reveals classified information.

00:32:22.674 --> 00:32:24.107
Starfleet could then request

00:32:24.175 --> 00:32:26.336
that it be recalled
for security purposes.

00:32:26.411 --> 00:32:27.742
Oh, great idea... a cover-up,

00:32:27.812 --> 00:32:29.211
and then everyone
will be convinced

00:32:29.280 --> 00:32:30.474
that it's a true story.

00:32:30.548 --> 00:32:32.482
Could we claim defamation?

00:32:32.550 --> 00:32:34.950
Well, we'd have to prove
that the story's about us

00:32:35.086 --> 00:32:36.951
and that we've been
harmed by it.

00:32:37.021 --> 00:32:40.957
We might win on those grounds,
but what about the Doctor?

00:32:41.059 --> 00:32:42.526
What about me, Captain?

00:32:42.593 --> 00:32:44.959
It's the crew's reputations
that are at risk.

00:32:45.096 --> 00:32:46.927
I'm not so sure.

00:32:46.998 --> 00:32:49.967
I think it's your reputation
that's on the line here.

00:32:50.068 --> 00:32:51.160
You have the same rights

00:32:51.235 --> 00:32:53.703
as every other member
of this crew,

00:32:53.771 --> 00:32:57.138
and I'm not going to let
this publisher say otherwise.

00:33:01.479 --> 00:33:05.245
Captain's Log, Stardate 54748.6.

00:33:05.316 --> 00:33:08.149
A Federation Arbitrator
has been assigned to determine

00:33:08.219 --> 00:33:09.481
whether the Doctor has the right

00:33:09.554 --> 00:33:11.920
to control
his artistic creation.

00:33:12.023 --> 00:33:14.082
Because of our limited com time
with Earth,

00:33:14.158 --> 00:33:17.184
the argument should take
about three days.

00:33:17.261 --> 00:33:19.525
You claim the Doctor
doesn't have the legal right

00:33:19.597 --> 00:33:21.258
to control this holoprogram.

00:33:21.332 --> 00:33:24.426
Yet you're promoting the fact
that Voyager's EMH wrote it.

00:33:24.502 --> 00:33:26.697
Our most successful
children's title

00:33:26.771 --> 00:33:30.172
is a program "written
by" Toby the targ.

00:33:30.241 --> 00:33:32.539
Fortunately, Toby
hasn't tried to stop me

00:33:32.610 --> 00:33:35.340
from distributing
any of his work.

00:33:35.413 --> 00:33:36.744
But you don't deny
that the Doctor

00:33:36.814 --> 00:33:38.179
is the creator
of this holonovel?

00:33:38.249 --> 00:33:42.583
No, but, uh, a replicator
created this cup of coffee.

00:33:42.653 --> 00:33:45.679
Should that replicator
be able to determine

00:33:45.757 --> 00:33:47.452
whether or not I can drink it?

00:33:47.525 --> 00:33:48.890
- I object!
- Doctor...

00:33:48.960 --> 00:33:51.224
An intriguing analogy,
Mr. Broht,

00:33:51.295 --> 00:33:54.128
but I have never encountered
a replicator

00:33:54.198 --> 00:33:57.964
that could compose music,
or paint landscapes,

00:33:58.102 --> 00:33:59.865
or perform microsurgery.

00:33:59.937 --> 00:34:01.165
Have you?

00:34:01.239 --> 00:34:02.900
Would you say
that you have a reputation

00:34:02.974 --> 00:34:06.910
for publishing respected,
original works of literature?

00:34:07.045 --> 00:34:08.706
I'd like to think so.

00:34:08.780 --> 00:34:11.078
And is the Doctor's holonovel
such a work?

00:34:11.149 --> 00:34:15.051
Actually, some aspects of it
are quite derivative.

00:34:15.119 --> 00:34:17.519
Has there even been
another work written

00:34:17.588 --> 00:34:19.579
about a hologram's
struggle for equality?

00:34:19.657 --> 00:34:22.717
Uh, not that I know of,
but, uh...

00:34:22.794 --> 00:34:24.659
Then, in that respect,
it is original.

00:34:24.729 --> 00:34:28.665
I suppose so.

00:34:28.733 --> 00:34:32.601
Your Honor, Section 7-Gamma
of the Twelfth Guarantee

00:34:32.670 --> 00:34:33.898
defines an artist

00:34:33.971 --> 00:34:36.337
as a "person who creates
an original artistic work."

00:34:36.407 --> 00:34:40.104
Mr. Broht admits that the Doctor
created this program

00:34:40.178 --> 00:34:41.611
and that it is original.

00:34:41.679 --> 00:34:43.237
I therefore submit

00:34:43.314 --> 00:34:44.838
that the Doctor
should be entitled

00:34:44.916 --> 00:34:45.940
to all rights and privileges

00:34:46.050 --> 00:34:48.541
accorded an artist
under the law.

00:34:52.356 --> 00:34:54.153
You've made a persuasive
argument, Commander,

00:34:54.225 --> 00:34:56.159
but there's a flaw
in your logic.

00:34:56.227 --> 00:34:59.162
As you point out, the law says

00:34:59.230 --> 00:35:01.698
that the creator of an artistic
work must be a "person."

00:35:01.766 --> 00:35:05.361
Your EMH doesn't meet
that criteria.

00:35:06.838 --> 00:35:09.602
Sir, we're losing the link.

00:35:09.674 --> 00:35:13.166
This hearing is in recess
until tomorrow.

00:35:16.180 --> 00:35:18.205
We're not doing well.

00:35:18.282 --> 00:35:20.614
Is that your considered
legal opinion?

00:35:20.685 --> 00:35:22.585
I'm sorry.

00:35:22.653 --> 00:35:24.848
It's just frustrating
to be told I have

00:35:24.922 --> 00:35:27.322
no more legal standing
than a replicator.

00:35:27.391 --> 00:35:29.689
If the Doctor doesn't
have the right

00:35:29.760 --> 00:35:31.227
to control his own work,

00:35:31.295 --> 00:35:33.263
then it stands to reason
he never had the right

00:35:33.331 --> 00:35:35.458
to enter into an agreement
with Mr. Broht.

00:35:35.533 --> 00:35:38.900
We could argue that the
original contract is invalid.

00:35:38.970 --> 00:35:41.268
In other words,
you want to concede

00:35:41.339 --> 00:35:43.204
that the Doctor isn't a person.

00:35:43.274 --> 00:35:45.299
What we need to do...

00:35:45.376 --> 00:35:48.573
is prove that he is
just as much a person

00:35:48.646 --> 00:35:49.943
as any of us.

00:35:50.014 --> 00:35:51.174
How do we do that?

00:35:51.249 --> 00:35:54.047
By telling your real life story.

00:35:57.922 --> 00:36:00.618
Because I was having difficulty
interacting with the crew,

00:36:00.691 --> 00:36:02.420
the Doctor gave me
social lessons.

00:36:02.493 --> 00:36:04.688
First, he taught me
how to make conversation.

00:36:04.762 --> 00:36:07.196
Later, how to dance.

00:36:07.265 --> 00:36:09.233
Eventually, he showed me

00:36:09.300 --> 00:36:11.495
how to express
romantic interest.

00:36:11.569 --> 00:36:13.298
Without his guidance,

00:36:13.371 --> 00:36:15.896
I wouldn't be the person
I am today.

00:36:16.007 --> 00:36:18.908
He decided it wasn't enough
to be just a doctor.

00:36:19.010 --> 00:36:21.911
So he added command subroutines
to his matrix,

00:36:21.979 --> 00:36:23.412
and now, in an emergency,

00:36:23.481 --> 00:36:25.915
he's as capable
as any Bridge officer.

00:36:26.017 --> 00:36:30.113
That only proves the Doctor's
program can be modified.

00:36:30.188 --> 00:36:31.416
Your Honor, I think it shows

00:36:31.489 --> 00:36:33.320
he has a desire
to become more than he is,

00:36:33.391 --> 00:36:35.256
just like any other person.

00:36:35.326 --> 00:36:37.123
He traveled halfway
across the galaxy

00:36:37.195 --> 00:36:40.926
to care for Lewis Zimmerman,
the man who created his program.

00:36:41.065 --> 00:36:42.191
It was like a son

00:36:42.266 --> 00:36:45.429
who wanted to show his father
what he'd become,

00:36:45.503 --> 00:36:47.528
so the old man
would be proud of him,

00:36:47.605 --> 00:36:51.200
and if you ask me... he was.

00:36:51.275 --> 00:36:53.266
I'd made myself clear,

00:36:53.344 --> 00:36:56.575
but the Doctor disobeyed
my direct orders.

00:36:56.647 --> 00:36:59.445
In the process,
he endangered the ship and crew.

00:36:59.517 --> 00:37:02.884
That's hardly
commendable behavior.

00:37:02.987 --> 00:37:04.579
No, it wasn't,

00:37:04.655 --> 00:37:06.748
but it was human.

00:37:06.824 --> 00:37:10.487
Starfleet had programmed him
to follow orders.

00:37:10.561 --> 00:37:12.893
The fact that he was capable
of doing otherwise

00:37:12.964 --> 00:37:15.956
proves that he can think
for himself.

00:37:17.034 --> 00:37:21.767
Your Honor, centuries ago,
in most places on Earth,

00:37:21.839 --> 00:37:25.275
only landowners
of a particular gender and race

00:37:25.343 --> 00:37:27.243
had any rights at all.

00:37:27.311 --> 00:37:31.042
Over time, those rights
were extended to all humans,

00:37:31.115 --> 00:37:33.743
and later,
as we explored the galaxy,

00:37:33.818 --> 00:37:36.446
to thousands
of other sentient species.

00:37:37.989 --> 00:37:40.822
Our definition of what
constitutes a person

00:37:40.891 --> 00:37:42.916
has continued to evolve.

00:37:43.060 --> 00:37:44.550
Now, we're asking

00:37:44.629 --> 00:37:47.359
that you expand that definition
once more...

00:37:47.431 --> 00:37:49.023
to include our Doctor.

00:37:49.100 --> 00:37:52.365
When I met him seven years ago,
I would never have believed

00:37:52.436 --> 00:37:56.668
that an EMH could become
a valued member of my crew...

00:37:56.741 --> 00:37:59.232
and my friend.

00:37:59.310 --> 00:38:02.575
The Doctor is a person

00:38:02.647 --> 00:38:06.515
as real as any flesh and blood
I have ever known.

00:38:06.584 --> 00:38:09.485
If you believe the testimony
you've heard here,

00:38:09.553 --> 00:38:12.181
it's only fair
to conclude that he has

00:38:12.256 --> 00:38:14.190
the same rights as any of us.

00:38:20.131 --> 00:38:22.065
You'll have my decision
in two days.

00:38:22.133 --> 00:38:24.499
This hearing is in recess.

00:38:36.047 --> 00:38:38.572
When you sing "Happy
Birthday" to your mother,

00:38:38.649 --> 00:38:40.173
try to stay in tune.

00:38:40.251 --> 00:38:41.377
What's this?

00:38:41.452 --> 00:38:43.784
An isolinear chip
entitling the bearer

00:38:43.854 --> 00:38:45.913
to three minutes
of com time tomorrow.

00:38:46.057 --> 00:38:47.183
You made it clear

00:38:47.258 --> 00:38:49.226
how important it was
to finish your conversation.

00:38:49.293 --> 00:38:51.591
I overreacted.

00:38:51.662 --> 00:38:54.130
I understand why you did.

00:38:54.198 --> 00:38:57.167
I've been observing the crew
interacting with their families

00:38:57.234 --> 00:38:58.929
over the past few days.

00:38:59.070 --> 00:39:00.799
It's become clear to me

00:39:00.871 --> 00:39:02.930
how meaningful
that communication can be.

00:39:06.577 --> 00:39:09.705
Thanks, but I can't accept this.

00:39:09.780 --> 00:39:11.247
Why not?

00:39:11.315 --> 00:39:13.340
You should contact
your own family.

00:39:13.417 --> 00:39:15.885
My parents were assimilated.

00:39:15.953 --> 00:39:17.545
Well, there must be someone.

00:39:17.621 --> 00:39:19.452
A distant relative?

00:39:19.523 --> 00:39:22.458
My father has a sister
on Earth...

00:39:22.526 --> 00:39:24.221
Irene Hansen.

00:39:24.295 --> 00:39:28.061
I'm sure she'd be thrilled
to hear from you.

00:39:32.603 --> 00:39:34.400
You can imagine my reaction

00:39:34.472 --> 00:39:37.305
when Starfleet told me
to expect your call.

00:39:37.375 --> 00:39:40.867
I experienced
some apprehension myself.

00:39:40.945 --> 00:39:43.277
You seem like
a lovely young woman,

00:39:43.347 --> 00:39:48.546
but you were the most stubborn
six year old I've ever met.

00:39:48.619 --> 00:39:52.055
Your parents left you with me
for a weekend,

00:39:52.123 --> 00:39:53.249
and you were so angry,

00:39:53.324 --> 00:39:55.315
you locked yourself
in my guest room

00:39:55.393 --> 00:39:56.826
and refused to come out.

00:39:58.362 --> 00:40:01.297
That must have been...
inconvenient for you.

00:40:01.365 --> 00:40:04.095
Oh, I coaxed you out
eventually...

00:40:04.168 --> 00:40:05.829
with a strawberry tart.

00:40:05.903 --> 00:40:08.269
I'm very fond of strawberries.

00:40:08.339 --> 00:40:10.603
I didn't realize
I'd eaten them as a child.

00:40:10.674 --> 00:40:12.266
You couldn't get enough of them.

00:40:13.344 --> 00:40:15.312
Of course, you didn't hesitate
to point out

00:40:15.379 --> 00:40:16.937
if they weren't perfectly ripe.

00:40:17.014 --> 00:40:20.916
I'm sorry if I insulted you.

00:40:20.985 --> 00:40:24.944
Perhaps I shouldn't have called.

00:40:25.089 --> 00:40:27.182
No, I'm very glad you did.

00:40:27.258 --> 00:40:31.661
It's wonderful
to see you again, Annika.

00:40:38.335 --> 00:40:40.428
We're exploring
new territory today,

00:40:40.504 --> 00:40:41.801
so it is fitting
that this hearing

00:40:41.872 --> 00:40:43.965
is being held at Pathfinder.

00:40:44.041 --> 00:40:46.339
The Doctor exhibits
many of the traits

00:40:46.410 --> 00:40:48.139
we associate with a person...

00:40:48.212 --> 00:40:52.876
intelligence, creativity,
ambition, even fallibility...

00:40:52.950 --> 00:40:54.884
but are these traits real

00:40:54.952 --> 00:40:57.944
or is the Doctor merely
programmed to simulate them?

00:40:58.022 --> 00:41:01.924
To be honest, I don't know.

00:41:02.059 --> 00:41:04.527
Eventually,
we will have to decide,

00:41:04.595 --> 00:41:06.620
because the issue
of holographic rights

00:41:06.697 --> 00:41:08.665
isn't going to go away.

00:41:08.732 --> 00:41:11.360
But at this time,
I am not prepared to rule

00:41:11.435 --> 00:41:13.528
that the Doctor
is a person under the law.

00:41:13.604 --> 00:41:16.835
However, it is obvious he is

00:41:16.907 --> 00:41:19.876
no ordinary hologram,
and while I can't say

00:41:19.944 --> 00:41:21.502
with certainty
that he is a person,

00:41:21.579 --> 00:41:23.479
I am willing to extend
the legal definition

00:41:23.547 --> 00:41:25.242
of artist to include the Doctor.

00:41:25.316 --> 00:41:26.578
I therefore rule

00:41:26.650 --> 00:41:28.550
that he has the right
to control his work,

00:41:28.619 --> 00:41:29.950
and I'm ordering all copies

00:41:30.054 --> 00:41:32.181
of his holonovels
to be recalled immediately.

00:41:32.256 --> 00:41:33.348
Your Honor...

00:41:33.424 --> 00:41:34.652
This hearing is adjourned.

00:41:34.725 --> 00:41:35.885
Congratulations, Doctor.

00:41:35.960 --> 00:41:37.325
Thank you, Reg.

00:41:37.394 --> 00:41:38.986
We'll be in touch, Captain.

00:41:41.365 --> 00:41:42.730
You don't look like someone

00:41:42.800 --> 00:41:45.894
who's just struck the first blow
for the rights of holograms.

00:41:45.970 --> 00:41:47.597
I don't mean
to sound ungrateful,

00:41:47.671 --> 00:41:51.607
but it feels like a hollow
victory... pardon the pun.

00:41:51.675 --> 00:41:54.473
The program's already been
played by thousands of people.

00:41:54.545 --> 00:41:55.477
The damage is done.

00:41:55.546 --> 00:41:56.535
We'll survive.

00:41:56.614 --> 00:41:57.774
If I'm not mistaken,

00:41:57.848 --> 00:42:00.078
don't you have a holonovel
to revise?

00:42:00.150 --> 00:42:02.880
Do you think I'll be able
to find another publisher?

00:42:02.953 --> 00:42:05.922
If there's one thing
I've learned about you, Doctor,

00:42:06.023 --> 00:42:07.388
it's that you can do
just about anything

00:42:07.458 --> 00:42:08.857
you set your mind to.

00:42:17.501 --> 00:42:19.799
Time for your diagnostic.

00:42:19.870 --> 00:42:21.929
Report to the Holo-Lab.

00:42:22.072 --> 00:42:23.699
I know the routine.

00:42:23.774 --> 00:42:29.508
And... while you're there,
do yourself a favor.

00:42:29.580 --> 00:42:33.346
Ask the operator
to run program 47-Beta.

00:42:33.417 --> 00:42:35.282
Why? What is it?

00:42:35.352 --> 00:42:38.219
It's called Photons Be Free.

00:42:38.289 --> 00:42:39.779
It's quite provocative.
